        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz remains the focal point of a contested closure dispute. Iran vows to keep the strait closed until the US meets its demands, per that claim's sourcing, while a separate report cited by a Chinese court settling a foreign-vessel collision dispute suggests ships have continued to move, leaving the physical transit picture unresolved. The site's carrier-posture assessment is a cautious closed, but that verdict is not settled fact. On the diplomatic track, 41NBC News reports Iran has laid out demands to reopen the strait; AP News, however, reports Iran will not reopen without US concessions, making the two characterisations contradictory and neither confirmed. Iran-Oman talks are ongoing according to Devdiscourse, though that report is unverified and single-source. Iran's Foreign Ministry, per Iranian state media IRNA (treat as a claim), denies the US has moral or legal standing to demand a reopening. Brent stood at $84.65, up 6.54% over the prior 24 hours, as of 11:53 UTC. The Hormuz Index Crisis Pressure is in the extreme band, flat over 24 hours, with physical transit deviation as top contributor; the Escalation Forecast sits in the elevated band and is rising, driven by Polymarket escalation contracts. The two indices diverge: current pressure is static while the forward signal is moving higher. PortWatch's most recent published day, 2026-08-02, recorded 2 transits against a pre-crisis baseline of 73 per day; that count is 8 days old and should not be read as current. Port arrivals across the Hormuz-area ports in the most recent 24 hours reached 468 vessels, a figure using a different methodology and not comparable to the PortWatch transit series.",
